In Linux, a child shell is a brand-new shell process spawned by a parent shell, running completely independently of its creator's local environment

While a subshell is a temporary "clone" that remembers everything from the parent, a child shell is like a fresh, blank canvas. It only knows about settings and variables that the parent shell explicitly decides to share with the rest of the system.

whatever modifications done in child shell will not be reflected in parent shell

How to Create a Child Shell

You explicitly create a child shell every time you call a shell program by its name from your current terminal, or when you run a standalone script:

# Launches a brand new Bash child shell
bash 

# Launches a different shell type (like Zsh or Dash) as a child
zsh 

# Running a script with a shebang (#!/bin/bash) also launches a child shell
./myscript.sh

Note: To close a child shell and return to your parent shell, you simply type exit.


Why Use a Child Shell? (Key Benefits)

• Total Environment Reset: It allows you to test scripts or configurations in a clean environment without your current terminal's history, local variables, or custom aliases interfering.
• Security & Cleanliness: If a script crashes, creates hundreds of temporary variables, or encounters an error, the chaos is completely contained. Once the script finishes, the child shell terminates, and the parent shell remains perfectly clean.
• Alternative Shell Testing: It lets you temporarily switch from Bash to another shell (like zsh or sh) to test compatibility.


Tracking Shell Depth: $SHLVL
Linux tracks how deep your child shells are nested using an environment variable called $SHLVL (Shell Level). Every time you launch a child shell, this number goes up by 1.

echo $SHLVL   # Outputs: 1 (Your main terminal)
bash          # Launches a child shell
echo $SHLVL   # Outputs: 2 (Inside the child shell)
exit          # Drops back down to the parent
echo $SHLVL   # Outputs: 1


The Crucial Rule: The export Command

Because a child shell is an independent process, it cannot see the parent shell's local variables unless you "export" them.

# 1. Create a standard local variable in the parent shell
MY_NAME="Alice"

# 2. Open a child shell
bash

# 3. Try to read it (It will print nothing!)
echo $MY_NAME 

# 4. Exit back to the parent shell
exit

# 5. Export the variable so the whole system can see it
export MY_NAME

# 6. Open a child shell again and read it
bash
echo $MY_NAME  # Outputs: Alice

