By default, shell scripts use a child shell

When you run a script (e.g., ./myscript.sh), Linux reads the "shebang" line at the very top (like #!/bin/bash), initializes a brand-new, independent child shell process, runs the code inside it, and terminates that child process when the script finishes.

Even though the script itself runs in a child shell, you can still use subshells inside the script's code for temporary tasks by using parentheses ( ) or command substitution $( ).
#!/bin/bash
# (This whole script is running in a CHILD SHELL)

# Creating a temporary SUBSHELL inside the script
(cd /tmp && touch test.txt)

# The script continues here, still in its original directory

